Session management
claude
Start interactive session in current directory
claude "query"
Open session with an initial prompt pre-loaded
claude -c
Continue most recent conversation in current dir
claude -r "name" "q"
Resume a named session by ID or name
claude --fork-session
Resume but create a new session ID (safe branch)
claude --from-pr 123
Resume sessions linked to a specific pull request
Non-interactive / piped (great for CI)
claude -p "query"
SDK/print mode — runs query then exits
cat file | claude -p "q"
Pipe file content directly into a prompt
claude --bare -p "q"
Skip hooks/MCP/CLAUDE.md — fastest for scripts
--max-turns 5
Cap agentic turns (safety for automated runs)
--max-budget-usd 2.00
Hard cost cap for print mode calls
--output-format json
Machine-readable output for pipelines
Model & effort control
--model sonnet
Use latest Sonnet (alias). Also: opus, claude-sonnet-4-6
--effort high
Set thinking depth: low | medium | high | xhigh | max
--fallback-model sonnet
Auto-fallback when primary model is overloaded
Permissions & safety
--allowedTools
Pre-approve tools (e.g. "Bash(git log *)" "Read")
--disallowedTools
Block tools from the model's context entirely
--permission-mode plan
Read-only mode — plan only, no writes
--dangerously-skip-permissions
Bypass all permission prompts (CI only, use carefully)
MCP & extensions
claude mcp
Configure MCP servers interactively
--mcp-config ./mcp.json
Load MCP server config from a JSON file
claude plugin install <name>
Install a plugin from the marketplace
claude agents
List all configured sub-agents
--add-dir ../lib ../apps
Grant file access to additional directories 

Six tabs covering everything you'll actually reach for as an AI architect. Here's how to get the most from it:
CLI Commands — the full command reference with flags organized by use case (sessions, piped/CI, model control, permissions, MCP).
Slash Commands — what you type inside an active session, including the Shift+Tab permission mode cycle which is gold for switching between planning-only and autonomous runs.
Architect Prompts — reusable prompt templates for architecture review, Terraform generation, debug analysis, documentation, and security scanning. These are structured to give Claude the context it needs upfront.
IaC / Terraform — prompts tailored for your Databricks/Azure work, including isolated DEV workspaces (matching your Unity Catalog isolation challenge), Service Bus pub/sub topology for EvA-style backends, and piping terraform plan output directly to Claude in CI.
Config & Hooks — the CLAUDE.md template I'd recommend for your project repos, plus the .claude/settings.json hook patterns for auto-linting after writes and secret-scanning before bash runs.
Shortcuts — keyboard shortcuts plus power one-liners: piping git diff --staged for commit messages, gh pr diff for PR review, and log-to-runbook for incident postmortems.
The Terraform pipe pattern (last tab) pairs especially well with your Azure DevOps pipelines — you can drop that terraform show -json | claude -p step right into a pipeline stage as a plan review gate.
